WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:13.700
You've probably asked yourself this question at least once: Can we truly have peace on a planet that's falling apart?

2
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.100
Good afternoon, everyone.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.900
My name's Matt Ko Yong, and let me give more context about my earlier assumption.

4
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:30.300
It's as bright as daylight that our world right now is changing by the minute beyond our imagination, and to be honest, that feels quite unsettling.

5
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:40.300
Every news cycle brings another heatwave, another flood, another conflict about scarce resources, and that makes me believe

6
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:47.000
that when we think about our future right now, we don't think of faster gadgets or cooler technology.

7
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.300
We think about something more fundamental.

8
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.000
We think about the people in two thousand forty-six being able to breathe clean air and drink clean water.

9
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.800
Of peace, peace for everyone.

10
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.400
But what does peace really mean?

11
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.600
According to the vision of humanity, peace doesn't just mean the absence of war.

12
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:18.000
It means the underlying condition that allows human potential to flourish, where societies can grow culturally, materially, and intellectually in a harmonious and stable way.

13
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
So here's the truth.

14
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.900
You can't have lasting peace on a planet that's falling apart.

15
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:32.900
Sustaining peace means giving people what they need in order to live with dignity: clean water, stable food supplies, safe shelter.

16
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:43.100
Climate action delivers exactly that, and strong global cooperation means sharing solutions towards climate action, such as the Paris Agreement adopted

17
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:48.300
in two thousand fifteen, when nations came together and commit themselves into cutting their emissions.

18
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.500
But treaties alone aren't enough.

19
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.500
What can we do to contribute?

20
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.200
Well, it might be easier than you think.

21
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.700
Take the bus or any other form of public transit at least one extra day of the week.

22
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.500
In your community, join local cleanups.

23
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.300
As a citizen, vote for people who speak out about our climate.

24
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.800
Every choice ripples forward.

25
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:18.300
Every degree we stabilize is another possibility that the people in two thousand forty-six are able to grow up and breathe clean air.

26
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:28.400
Because if we come together as one global community, we can do something extraordinary: have a sustainable future that balances peace and climate stability at the same

27
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.400
time.

28
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:29.900
Thank you.

